   "source": [
    "# Trajectory and velocity analysis of scRNAseq COLON data "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This is one in the series of notebooks presenting the data analysis done in the study \"Widespread epithelial dedifferentiation in patients with ulcerative colitis\". It is also intended as a tutorial in scRNAseq analysis. Please refer to the REAMDE file for the description of different parts of the tutorial and how to follow it.  "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 1. Data load and quality control"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This is the first part of the guide in which we load the data files and perform a throughout quality control of the cells. We discard the cells of low quality (as determined by one of many factors) and very lowly expressed genes. At the end we pack all our sample files into one, so in the the next parts we may work on our whole dataset jointly. If you need help getting your data into the right format or dowloading the packages, please refer to the **Part 0** of this tutorial. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Healthy dataset"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This notebook shows the pipeline done on the \"Healthy dataset\" (see study for details regarding samples constituting the dataset). Here I am explaining the procedure step by step, so hopefully one can repeat it on their own data. In this repository there are also notebooks showing the same pipeline for other datasets from the study. I am not repeating the descriptions in those, so please always refer to the \"Healthy dataset\" notebooks while following the tutorial. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Here I am working on 4 samples which I want to merge together into one condition - Healthy. You need to adjust the code accordingly to the number of samples/conditions you're working on. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 0. Imports and settings"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import scanpy as sc\n",
    "import scvelo as scv\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sb\n",
    "import scrublet as scr\n",
    "import doubletdetection"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "toc-hr-collapsed": true,
    "toc-nb-collapsed": true
   },
   "source": [
    "#### 1. Load data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "All files are in the `.loom` format. They have been preprcoessed with *cellranger* (demultiplexing, alignment and counting) and *velocyto* (annotation of spliced/unspliced counts). "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "After loading in the file, we have to remove some of the unnecssary data fields generated by previous processing. What we want in the end is just the matrix of raw counts, and matrices of spliced and unspliced reads."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stderr",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Variable names are not unique. To make them unique, call `.var_names_make_unique`.\n",
      "Variable names are not unique. To make them unique, call `.var_names_make_unique`.\n",
      "Variable names are not unique. To make them unique, call `.var_names_make_unique`.\n",
      "Variable names are not unique. To make them unique, call `.var_names_make_unique`.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "sample_name = \"healthy1\"\n",
    "file = \"data/{}.loom\".format(sample_name)\n",
    "adata1 = scv.read_loom(file, sparse=True, cleanup=True)\n",
    "adata1.var_names_make_unique()\n",
    "# Delete obsolite data created by velocyto\n",
    "del adata1.obs['Clusters']\n",
    "del adata1.obs['_X']\n",
    "del adata1.obs['_Y']\n",
    "del adata1.var['Accession']\n",
    "del adata1.var['Chromosome']\n",
    "del adata1.var['End']\n",
    "del adata1.var['Start']\n",
    "del adata1.var['Strand']\n",
    "del adata1.layers['ambiguous']\n",
    "\n",
    "sample_name = \"healthy2\"\n",
    "file = \"data/{}.loom\".format(sample_name)\n",
    "adata2 = scv.read_loom(file, sparse=True, cleanup=True)\n",
    "adata2.var_names_make_unique()\n",
    "del adata2.obs['Clusters']\n",
    "del adata2.obs['_X']\n",
    "del adata2.obs['_Y']\n",
    "del adata2.var['Accession']\n",
    "del adata2.var['Chromosome']\n",
    "del adata2.var['End']\n",
    "del adata2.var['Start']\n",
    "del adata2.var['Strand']\n",
    "del adata2.layers['ambiguous']\n",
    "\n",
    "sample_name = \"healthy3\"\n",
    "file = \"data/{}.loom\".format(sample_name)\n",
    "adata3 = scv.read_loom(file, sparse=True, cleanup=True)\n",
    "adata3.var_names_make_unique()\n",
    "del adata3.obs['Clusters']\n",
    "del adata3.obs['_X']\n",
    "del adata3.obs['_Y']\n",
    "del adata3.var['Accession']\n",
    "del adata3.var['Chromosome']\n",
    "del adata3.var['End']\n",
    "del adata3.var['Start']\n",
    "del adata3.var['Strand']\n",
    "del adata3.layers['ambiguous']\n",
    "\n",
    "sample_name = \"healthy4\"\n",
    "file = \"data/{}.loom\".format(sample_name)\n",
    "adata4 = scv.read_loom(file, sparse=True, cleanup=True)\n",
    "adata4.var_names_make_unique()\n",
    "del adata4.obs['Clusters']\n",
    "del adata4.obs['_X']\n",
    "del adata4.obs['_Y']\n",
    "del adata4.var['Accession']\n",
    "del adata4.var['Chromosome']\n",
    "del adata4.var['End']\n",
    "del adata4.var['Start']\n",
    "del adata4.var['Strand']\n",
    "del adata4.layers['ambiguous']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "toc-hr-collapsed": true,
    "toc-nb-collapsed": true
   },
   "source": [
    "#### 2. Quality control"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The first step of the analysis is to filter the cells based on their quality. This will remove dead cells, doublets and other sequencing artifacts. This is necessary as large amount of low quality data points could influence downstream results. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Quality of a cell can be determined based on a number of metrics:\n",
    "- number of counts (also unspliced when working with RNA velocity)\n",
    "- number of genes\n",
    "- percent of mitochondrail counts \n",
    "- percent of ribosomal counts\n",
    "\n",
    "The last point is not used as a metric here, but can be, depending on the origin of your dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now we need to decide on a cutoff treshold for each of the parameters. A naive aproach would be to join the samples together first and then set a treshold common to all the samples (for example not more than 5% mito counts per cell). This may be easier, however there may be large differences between the samples in distribution of those quality metrics. In which case it is prefferable to choose filtering gates separately for each of the samples. That way we don't discriminate against any of the samples. \n",
    "\n",
    "First thing to do is to inspect the distribution per sample of the common quality metrics. We temporarily merge samples together in order to easier compare the samples. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 930.6x400 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {
      "image/png": {
       "height": 367,
       "width": 729
      },
      "needs_background": "light"
     },
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 930.6x400 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {
      "image/png": {
       "height": 367,
       "width": 716
      },
      "needs_background": "light"
     },
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 930.6x400 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {
      "image/png": {
       "height": 367,
       "width": 738
      },
      "needs_background": "light"
     },
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Concatenate the files to calculate joint QC metrics\n",
    "adata_qc = adata1.concatenate(adata2, adata3, adata4, \n",
    "                              batch_key='Sample', batch_categories=['healthy1', 'healthy2', 'healthy3', 'healthy4'])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Calculate QC covariates\n",
    "adata_qc.obs['n_counts'] = adata_qc.X.sum(1)\n",
    "adata_qc.obs['n_spliced'] = adata_qc.layers['spliced'].sum(1)\n",
    "adata_qc.obs['n_unspliced'] = adata_qc.layers['unspliced'].sum(1)\n",
    "adata_qc.obs['n_genes'] = (adata_qc.X > 0).sum(1)\n",
    "# Compute for each cell the fraction of counts in mitochondrial genes vs. all genes\n",
    "mito_genes = adata_qc.var_names.str.startswith('MT-')\n",
    "adata_qc.obs['percent_mito'] = np.sum(\n",
    "    adata_qc[:, mito_genes].X, axis=1) / np.sum(adata_qc.X, axis=1)\n",
    "# Compute for each cell the fraction of counts in ribosomal genes vs. all genes\n",
    "ribo_genes = adata_qc.var_names.str.startswith('RP')\n",
    "adata_qc.obs['percent_ribo'] = np.sum(\n",
    "    adata_qc[:, ribo_genes].X, axis=1) / np.sum(adata_qc.X, axis=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Initial plotting settings for more detailed scatter and dist plots.\n",
    "scv.settings.set_figure_params('scvelo', dpi=100, vector_friendly=True, figsize=[4,4])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot the statistics on a violin plot split by sample.\n",
    "sc.pl.violin(adata_qc, ['n_genes', 'n_counts'], jitter=0.4, groupby='Sample')\n",
    "sc.pl.violin(adata_qc, ['percent_mito', 'percent_ribo'], jitter=0.4, groupby='Sample')\n",
    "sc.pl.violin(adata_qc, ['n_spliced', 'n_unspliced'], jitter=0.4, groupby='Sample')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As we can see in the figure above, there are significant differences in the distributions of quality metrics in the four samples. That is why we need to adjust the filtering thresholds per sample. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### 2.1 healthy1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We begin with the sample *healthy1*. First, we calculate and visualise the quality metrics again, this time focusing on the single sample."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 313.75x200 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {
      "image/png": {
       "height": 235,
       "width": 297
      },
      "needs_background": "light"
     },
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 288.1x200 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {
      "image/png": {
       "height": 215,
       "width": 254
      },
      "needs_background": "light"
     },
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Calculate QC covariates\n",
    "adata1.obs['n_counts'] = adata1.X.sum(1)\n",
    "adata1.obs['log_counts'] = np.log(adata1.obs['n_counts'])\n",
    "adata1.obs['n_spliced'] = adata1.layers['spliced'].sum(1)\n",
    "adata1.obs['n_unspliced'] = adata1.layers['unspliced'].sum(1)\n",
    "adata1.obs['n_genes'] = (adata1.X > 0).sum(1)\n",
    "# Compute for each cell the fraction of counts in mito genes vs. all genes\n",
    "mito_genes = adata1.var_names.str.startswith('MT-')\n",
    "adata1.obs['percent_mito'] = np.sum(\n",
    "    adata1[:, mito_genes].X, axis=1) / np.sum(adata1.X, axis=1)\n",
    "# Compute for each cell the fraction of counts in ribosomal genes vs. all genes\n",
    "ribo_genes = adata1.var_names.str.startswith('RP')\n",
    "adata1.obs['percent_ribo'] = np.sum(\n",
    "    adata1[:, ribo_genes].X, axis=1) / np.sum(adata1.X, axis=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Decrease the size of plots\n",
    "scv.settings.set_figure_params('scvelo', dpi=100, vector_friendly=True, figsize=[3,2])\n",
    "\n",
    "sc.pl.scatter(adata1, 'n_counts', 'n_genes', color='percent_mito', title='Percent mitochondrial (colour)')\n",
    "sc.pl.scatter(adata1, 'n_spliced', 'n_unspliced')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "There is a strong correlation between the number of counts, genes and the mitochondrial content, however, it is still important to look at those statistics jointly. There are many outliers (high number of counts, low number of genes). Specifically, we can see cells with many genes (likely not dead) that have a high mitochondrial content (around 50%). This means we cannot simply exclude all the cells with more than 5% mito content.\n",
    "\n",
    "Looking at the second we can see the see the relation between the number of spliced and unspliced counts. Importantly, a number of cells have a notably lower amount of unspliced counts compared to the spliced ones. Normally those cells would be fine to keep, but in the case when we are working with RNA velocity, a cell with low number of unspliced counts can negatively imapct the results. We will remove some of those cells in a moment.  \n",
    "\n",
    "It is also important to consider single results from the perspective of the entire population. In this example we can see a few cells with a very high number of counts - over 2 times larger than average. Even though in a different dataset those cells could be fine, here we consider them as outliers, probably doublets (two cells sequenced together). "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Presence of doublets in the dataset can be infered. Here we do it with two tools - `scrublet` and `DoubletDetection`. It is important to detect the doublets **before** we remove any of the low quality cells, as the tools need a full picture of the dataset to work properly. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "First we use the tooll called `scrublet` to identify doublets - two cells barcoded togheter. Especially important when we are trying too identify cells transitioning between celltypes. We use scrublet to assign doublet score (probability of being a doublet)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The parameters should be fine by defualt, but you may want/need to adjust them. Please refer to the [official documentation](https://github.com/AllonKleinLab/scrublet/blob/master/examples/scrublet_basics.ipynb)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "scrolled": true
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Doublet analysis\n",
    "scrub = scr.Scrublet(adata1.X, expected_doublet_rate=0.06)\n",
    "doublet_scores, predicted_doublets = scrub.scrub_doublets(min_counts=2, \n",
    "                                                          min_cells=3, \n",
    "                                                          min_gene_variability_pctl=85, \n",
    "                                                          n_prin_comps=30,\n",
    "                                                          mean_center=False, \n",
    "                                                          normalize_variance=False,\n",
    "                                                          log_transform=True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`scrublet` simulates doublets by artifically combining cells from the dataset and then assigns a doublet score to both (observed cells and simulated doublet cells). Based on those it sets a treshold at doublet score to predict doublets. We can compare those results in a histogram. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "scrolled": false
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "scrub.plot_histogram()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can also quickly plot the doublets on a UMAP plot to see that they cluster together. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "scrub.set_embedding('UMAP', scr.get_umap(scrub.manifold_obs_, 10, min_dist=0.3))\n",
    "scrub.plot_embedding('UMAP', order_points=True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Looking at the cells in clusters with high doublet score we can assume there are probably more doublets than only those predicted by `scrublet`. Later we will see similar results comparing them with the next tool. It is therefore better to filter out the doublets ourselves based on the score. \n",
    "\n",
    "We assign the doublet score to a variable in the `adata` object."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata1.obs['doublet_score'] = scrub.doublet_scores_obs_"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Another tool to annotate doublets is `DoubletDetection`. More sophisticated, uses machine learning, but takes a **long** time to run (5-10 min).  Uses a classsifier and does not need us to supply a cut-off value for being a doublet. \n",
    "\n",
    "Removing cells annotated as doublets by those two tools ensures our later results are not affected by those technical errors. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "clf = doubletdetection.BoostClassifier(n_iters=50, use_phenograph=False, standard_scaling=True)\n",
    "doublets = clf.fit(adata1.X).predict(p_thresh=1e-16, voter_thresh=0.5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can see how the number of predicted doublets changes per iteration of the algorithm as the doublet prediction threshold is refined. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "f = doubletdetection.plot.convergence(clf, show=True, p_thresh=1e-16, voter_thresh=0.5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Again, we can plot the predicted doublets on a umap plot."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "f2, umap_coords = doubletdetection.plot.umap_plot(adata1.X, doublets, random_state=1, show=True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now we add the predicted doublets as another variable."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "adata1.obs['doublet'] = list(map(str, map(int, doublets)))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Next, we look at distribution plots of **count** statistics to set thresholds for **cell filtering**. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Thresholding decision: spliced counts\n",
    "ax=sb.distplot(adata1.obs['n_spliced'], kde=False)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Closer look at lower treshold\n",
    "ax=sb.distplot(adata1.obs['n_spliced'][adata1.obs['n_spliced']<3500], kde=False, bins=60)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Closer look at upper treshold\n",
    "ax=sb.distplot(adata1.obs['n_spliced'][adata1.obs['n_spliced']>15000], kde=False, bins=60)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Statistically, the distribution of counts we observe should approach a Gaussian (normal) distribution. By visually tracing the characteristic bell-shaped curve, we can approximate the cut-off threshold for high quality cells. We do it both for data points with too few or too many counts. The first occur e.g. when debree is sequnced rather than full cells, and the latter are two (or more) cells sequenced at once. \n",
    "\n",
    "In this case we can see that the lower cut-off should be somewhere between 1000 and 1500 cells and the upper cut-off should be around 18000 counts. You can make more plots with different level of \"zoom\" to determine the values more precise. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In a similar fashion we now look at the unspliced counts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#Thresholding decision: unspliced counts\n",
    "ax=sb.distplot(adata1.obs['n_unspliced'], kde=False)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "ax=sb.distplot(adata1.obs['n_unspliced'][adata1.obs['n_unspliced']<2000], kde=False, bins=60)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Too many unspliced counts shouldn't be an issue on its own, so we only look at the lower threshold. \n",
    "\n",
    "This time the curve shape is not as obvious to follow. We stay on the permissive side and set the cut-off the 600."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Finally, we look at distribution of the number of genes per cell to set the final gate for cell filtering."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#Thresholding decision: genes\n",
    "ax=sb.distplot(adata1.obs['n_genes'], kde=False, bins=60)\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "ax=sb.distplot(adata1.obs['n_genes'][adata1.obs['n_genes']<1000], kde=False, bins=60)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now we apply the estimated limits and filter out low quality cells. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
